<DocsLayout title="Chudware documentation" description="Choose a desktop setup guide or build from source; then learn modeling, simulation, CAM, PLM, and the desktop copilot." section="Start here">
  <h2>Build something real</h2>
  <p>The guides follow the shape of a real project: get set up, sketch and constrain geometry, assemble parts, rebuild from scans or photos, prepare CAM, and keep the project traceable.</p>
  <p>First choose a desktop setup guide for <a href="/docs/install-windows/">Windows</a>, <a href="/docs/install-linux/">Linux</a>, or <a href="/docs/install-macos/">macOS</a>, then continue to <a href="/docs/first-part/">your first part</a>. The software is MIT licensed and free to build from the public repository. Public desktop installers are not yet available.</p>
  <h2>Rebuild from source evidence</h2>
  <p>The <a href="/docs/reverse-engineering/">reverse engineering guide</a> covers photos, video, scan meshes, datasheets, and library records, with explicit scale confirmation, dimension review, approximate-result labels, deviation, and provenance.</p>
  <h2>Use the copilot as a collaborator</h2>
  <p>With a configured desktop runtime and your own AI provider account or API key, a team of agents can collaborate across the workspace and leave a human-editable feature history with relations and critical dimensions. Review the feature tree and measurements: the copilot does not replace engineering judgment. See the <a href="/docs/ai-copilot/">AI setup and limits</a>.</p>
  <h2>Keep help close</h2>
  <p>Browse the guides by topic or search for a tool, command, or workflow.</p>
</DocsLayout>
